  "strategy": "A novel approach to the Erdos minimum overlap problem is presented, combining geometric optimization with reinforcement learning-inspired perturbations. The key innovation is using dimensionality reduction to 1D representations while preserving integral constraints, coupled with a dynamic schedule for real-time gradient-based improvements.\n\nAlgorithm Design:\n1. Dimensionality Reduction:\n   - Project input to 1D using careful scaling (preserve integral)\n   - For (params): time-based resolution adjustments, guaranteed feasibility\n2. Gradient Calculation:\n   - Compute precise overlap gradient using correlation numpy\n   - Handle numerical stability with clipping and bounds\n3. Perturbation Strategies:\n   - Use \"worst-shift\" detection\n   -Apply structured binary swaps\n   -Use adaptive parameter scheduling\n4. Execution Phases:\n   -Initial repair optimization\n   -Resolution scaling by 2x\n   -Dense grid refinement\n   -Final polish with local adjustments\n\nImplementation Details:\n1. Variables:\n   -n_points: grid size (default 310)\n   -dx = 2.0/n_points\n   -Integral constraint: sum(h) = n_points/2\n2. Objective:\n   -max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = (1/dx) * max_lag(correlation)\n3. Repair Mechanism:\n   -Conservative clipping\n   -Proportional value adjustment\n   -Position-aware binary editing\n4. Heuristics:\n   -Restart when stuck (explore_pattern=True)\n   -Adaptive resolution scaling\n   -Fallback mechanism (geometric mean)\n5. Safeguards:\n   -Tolerance-based convergence checks\n   -Gradient smoothing\n   -Float32/float64 enforcement\n6. DimensionHandling:\n   -Grid scaling: linear interpolation\n   -Constraints: projected gradient descent\n7. Evaluation:\n   -Real-time correlation check\n   -Objective tracking\n   -Performance comparison\n\nComplexity:\n-O(n^2) correlation computations\n-L1 constraints through projection\n-Time: 1100 seconds budget breakdown\n   -Repair: ~200s\n   -Binary swaps: ~600s\n   -Resolution: ~300s\n\nFallback:\ndef fallback(n_points):\n    h = np.zeros(n_points)\n    for i in range(n_points):\n        h[i] = 0.5  + 0.2 * np.sin(4*i/np.pi)\n    return h.clip(0,1), np.NaN, n_points\n\nObjective check:\nassert 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\nassert abs(sum(h) - n_points/2) < tolerance\n\nThis approach leverages gradient information more effectively than previous SA implementations while maintaining global exploration through controlled dimensionality changes. The phased execution ensures we escape local minima without excessive computation.",
